@NODE S2 "8.2. Introducing sgmllib.py"
@TITLE "8.2. Introducing sgmllib.py"
@NEXT "S3"
@PREV "S1"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}8.2. Introducing @{code}sgmllib.py@{body}@{ub}

HTML processing is broken into three steps: breaking down the HTML into its constituent pieces, fiddling with the pieces, and reconstructing the pieces into HTML again. The first step is done by @{code}sgmllib.py@{body}, a part of the standard Python library.

The key to understanding this chapter is to realize that HTML is not just text, it is structured text. The structure is derived from the more-or-less-hierarchical sequence of start tags and end tags. Usually you don't work with HTML this way; you work with it @{i}textually@{ui} in a text editor, or @{i}visually@{ui} in a web browser or web authoring tool. @{code}sgmllib.py@{body} presents HTML @{i}structurally@{ui}.

@{code}sgmllib.py@{body} contains one important class: @{code}SGMLParser@{body}. @{code}SGMLParser@{body} parses HTML into useful pieces, like start tags and end tags. As soon as it succeeds in breaking down some data into a useful piece, it calls a method on itself based on what it found. In order to use the parser, you subclass the @{code}SGMLParser@{body} class and override these methods. This is what I meant when I said that it presents HTML @{i}structurally@{ui}: the structure of the HTML determines the sequence of method calls and the arguments passed to each method.

@{code}SGMLParser@{body} parses HTML into 8 kinds of data, and calls a separate method for each of them:

Start tag An HTML tag that starts a block, like @{code}@{body}, @{code}@{body}, @{code}@{body}, or @{code}@{body}, or a standalone tag like @{code}@{body} or @{code}@{body}. When it finds a start tag @{i}@{code}tagname@{body}@{ui}, @{code}SGMLParser@{body} will look for a method called @{code}start_@{i}@{code}tagname@{body}@{ui}@{body} or @{code}do_@{i}@{code}tagname@{body}@{ui}@{body}. For instance, when it finds a @{code}@{body} tag, it will look for a @{code}start_pre@{body} or @{code}do_pre@{body} method. If found, @{code}SGMLParser@{body} calls this method with a list of the tag's attributes; otherwise, it calls @{code}unknown_starttag@{body} with the tag name and list of attributes. End tag An HTML tag that ends a block, like @{code}@{body}, @{code}@{body}, @{code}@{body}, or @{code}@{body}. When it finds an end tag, @{code}SGMLParser@{body} will look for a method called @{code}end_@{i}@{code}tagname@{body}@{ui}@{body}. If found, @{code}SGMLParser@{body} calls this method, otherwise it calls @{code}unknown_endtag@{body} with the tag name. Character reference An escaped character referenced by its decimal or hexadecimal equivalent, like @{code} @{body}. When found, @{code}SGMLParser@{body} calls @{code}handle_charref@{body} with the text of the decimal or hexadecimal character equivalent. Entity reference An HTML entity, like @{code}&copy;@{body}. When found, @{code}SGMLParser@{body} calls @{code}handle_entityref@{body} with the name of the HTML entity. Comment An HTML comment, enclosed in @{code}@{body}. When found, @{code}SGMLParser@{body} calls @{code}handle_comment@{body} with the body of the comment. Processing instruction An HTML processing instruction, enclosed in @{code}@{body}. When found, @{code}SGMLParser@{body} calls @{code}handle_pi@{body} with the body of the processing instruction. Declaration An HTML declaration, such as a @{code}DOCTYPE@{body}, enclosed in @{code}@{body}. When found, @{code}SGMLParser@{body} calls @{code}handle_decl@{body} with the body of the declaration. Text data A block of text. Anything that doesn't fit into the other 7 categories. When found, @{code}SGMLParser@{body} calls @{code}handle_data@{body} with the text.

Python 2.0 had a bug where @{code}SGMLParser@{body} would not recognize declarations at all (@{code}handle_decl@{body} would never be called), which meant that @{code}DOCTYPE@{body} s were silently ignored. This is fixed in Python 2.1.

@{code}sgmllib.py@{body} comes with a test suite to illustrate this. You can run @{code}sgmllib.py@{body}, passing the name of an HTML file on the command line, and it will print out the tags and other elements as it parses them. It does this by subclassing the @{code}SGMLParser@{body} class and defining @{code}unknown_starttag@{body}, @{code}unknown_endtag@{body}, @{code}handle_data@{body} and other methods which simply print their arguments.

In the ActivePython IDE on Windows, you can specify command line arguments in the "Run script" dialog. Separate multiple arguments with spaces.

@{b}Example 8.4. Sample test of @{code}sgmllib.py@{body}@{ub}

Here is a snippet from the table of contents of the HTML version of this book. Of course your paths may vary. (If you haven't downloaded the HTML version of the book, you can do so at http://diveintopython.org/.

@{code}

c:\\python23\\lib> type "c:\\downloads\\diveintopython\\html\\toc\\index.html"

Dive Into Python

... rest of file omitted for brevity ...

@{body}

Running this through the test suite of @{code}sgmllib.py@{body} yields this output:

@{code}

c:\\python23\\lib> python sgmllib.py "c:\\downloads\\diveintopython\\html\\toc\\index.html"
data: '\\n\\n'
start tag:
data: '\\n '
start tag:
data: '\\n '
start tag:
data: '\\n \\n '
start tag:
data: 'Dive Into Python'
end tag:
data: '\\n '
start tag:
data: '\\n '

... rest of output omitted for brevity ...

@{body}

Here's the roadmap for the rest of the chapter:

* Subclass @{code}SGMLParser@{body} to create classes that extract interesting data out of HTML documents.

* Subclass @{code}SGMLParser@{body} to create @{code}BaseHTMLProcessor@{body}, which overrides all 8 handler methods and uses them to reconstruct the original HTML from the pieces.

* Subclass @{code}BaseHTMLProcessor@{body} to create @{code}Dialectizer@{body}, which adds some methods to process specific HTML tags specially, and overrides the @{code}handle_data@{body} method to provide a framework for processing the text blocks between the HTML tags.

* Subclass @{code}Dialectizer@{body} to create classes that define text processing rules used by @{code}Dialectizer.handle_data@{body}.

* Write a test suite that grabs a real web page from @{code}http://diveintopython.org/@{body} and processes it.

Along the way, you'll also learn about @{code}locals@{body}, @{code}globals@{body}, and dictionary-based string formatting.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S3 "8.3. Extracting data from HTML documents"
